    1 2ABC              ; runtime.asm -- what the 8080 does not have: multiplication, division and the shifts by a
    2 2ABC              ; variable count, sixteen bits and thirty-two.
    3 2ABC              ;
    4 2ABC              ; The compiler expands what it can inline and calls in here for the rest, under the ordinary
    5 2ABC              ; compiler's calling convention: a sixteen-bit operation takes its operands in HL and DE
    6 2ABC              ; and returns in HL; a thirty-two-bit one takes the first operand's low word in HL and high word
    7 2ABC              ; in DE and the second's in BC and on the stack above the return address (or a count in BC), and
    8 2ABC              ; returns in HL (low) and DE (high). So these are ordinary functions as far as the compiler is
    9 2ABC              ; concerned.
   10 2ABC              ;
   11 2ABC              ; Only what a program uses is assembled: every routine is inside IFUSED, so an image carries the
   12 2ABC              ; routines it calls and the ones those call, and nothing else. sjasmplus decides that in one pass
   13 2ABC              ; only if every routine comes after all the routines that call it -- the order below -- which is
   14 2ABC              ; also why the thirty-two-bit routines, which call the sixteen-bit ones, come first. They used to
   15 2ABC              ; be in runtime32.asm; that file is now empty and kept so that command lines naming it still work.
   16 2ABC              ;
   17 2ABC              ; Division of longs keeps its operands in static memory, because it needs a dividend, a divisor
   18 2ABC              ; and a remainder of four bytes each and the machine has three pairs, and so does the floating
   19 2ABC              ; point. An interrupt handler gets a copy of its own of this whole file, every name of it NAME__i,
   20 2ABC              ; which x80cc makes when it links a program whose handlers call into here: the handler never runs
   21 2ABC              ; inside the program's routines, nor in their working space. What the two share is between a line ";@shared" and one ";@end", which the
   22 2ABC              ; copy leaves out.
   23 2ABC              ;
   24 2ABC              ; Sixty-four-bit arithmetic takes its operands by address, the result written over the first
   25 2ABC              ; operand, since eight bytes fit in no set of registers; see the comment above those routines.
   26 2ABC              ;
   27 2ABC              ; Written by hand and measured rather than guessed at: every routine is checked against the same
   28 2ABC              ; operations computed in C by the compiler's own interpreter.
   29 2ABC
   30 2ABC              ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   31 2ABC              ; binary64: double arithmetic, comparison, and conversions to and from float, long and long long.
   32 2ABC              ;
   33 2ABC              ; A double goes by address: an operation takes a's address in HL and b's in DE and writes the
   34 2ABC              ; result over a; a conversion from a double reads it through HL; one to a double writes it through
   35 2ABC              ; HL and takes what it converts in DE (low word) and BC (high word), or in place for a long long.
   36 2ABC              ; The algorithms are those of the compiler's C reference implementation of IEEE 754, as for float
   37 2ABC              ; below, and the results agree with it bit for bit.
   38 2ABC              ;
   39 2ABC              ; Eight bytes fit in no set of registers, so a number in the middle of an operation is in memory,
   40 2ABC              ; in the sixty-four-bit arithmetic's qa, qb and qr below, and moved about by its routines a byte at
   41 2ABC              ; a time: compact rather than fast, and still several times faster than the C was. A finite number
   42 2ABC              ; there is a sign (in dsg), an exponent (in DE, or dexp) and a significand of sixty-four bits with
   43 2ABC              ; its leading one at bit 63 -- fifty-three bits of the result and eleven below them, the lowest a
   44 2ABC              ; sticky bit. Its value is significand / 2^63 * 2^(exponent - 1023). __x80_d_rpack makes the
   45 2ABC              ; double of that and writes it where the result goes (qpa).
